From 8d156e5fd3b981320f0b948595b1b9f1f209f772 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TorrServerManager <noreply@torrservermanager.local>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 23:42:52 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 07/12] gstreamer: bound task-slot contention instead of
 letting clients evict each other
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A hash owns exactly one task slot, so two clients wanting different files of the same
torrent contend for it. GetOrAdd looped without a bound, and its singleflight key was
the hash alone, so one caller could be handed a task it never asked for and go round
again — each iteration tearing down a live pipeline and paying for an uncancellable
45s cue read.

Three changes:

  - the retry loop is bounded and context-aware, and reports ErrTaskBusy (503 with
    Retry-After) instead of spinning;
  - the singleflight key is the whole request, so unrelated requests stop collapsing
    into each other's results;
  - a task that was itself just installed and is still being served is defended from
    eviction for a short grace period. The signal is deliberately CreatedAt and not
    just LastActive: a task the viewer has been watching has an old CreatedAt and is
    still evicted immediately, so switching episodes stays instant, while two clients
    trading the slot get throttled to one swap per grace period.

The cue timeline is now cached and reads on the caller's context, so a disconnected
client stops paying for range requests and a refused swap costs nothing at all — the
defence is checked before probing.
